The expression we got for $\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ isn't in terms of only $x$ and $y$ because it includes $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ in it.

Let's plug the original equation $\blueD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\dfrac xy}$ in the new equation to obtain an expression in terms of only $x$ and $y$:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}&=\dfrac{y-\blueD{\dfrac{dy}{dx}}\cdot x}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{y-\blueD{\dfrac xy}\cdot x}{y^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{y^2-x^2}{y^3}
\end{align}$
